\title{\bf Fingerprinting Language-Model Weights via the Distributional Divergence of\\ Transferable Low-Rank Probes}

\author{Hanlin Tan}

\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Determining whether two language models have (nearly) identical weights is
central to model-copy detection, provenance attribution, and adversarial
weight-tampering auditing, but is difficult because hidden states are
inaccessible in production and because two models can produce identical outputs
while differing in their parameters. We introduce a two-stage approach built on
an observable, distribution-level signal. First, we generate
\emph{representation-aligned probes}: sentences the model's own intermediate
representation places near a common reference, so the probe set stays low-rank
and concentrated across models, though the sentences differ on the surface.
They are produced by a HotFlip-style discrete
optimization over a shared subword vocabulary, a one-time \emph{white-box} step
on a local open-weight model. Second, we measure their mean per-probe
\emph{distributional divergence} (KL) of the induced next-token distributions
as a fingerprint. Detection is \emph{black-box}, requiring only the top-$k$
log-probabilities of the served model.

The fingerprint is a monotone, sensitive detector of weight perturbation:
adding Gaussian noise of scale $r\sigma$ to three open-weight models
(Qwen3.5-0.8B, Llama-3.2-1B, Gemma-3-1B) yields calibration curves whose
divergence grows quadratically in $r$, consistent with the Fisher-information
prediction and stable across model families and the two Qwen layers
($L8$/$L16$). A zero-noise determinism check over the DeepSeek API confirms
that the black-box readout is deterministic end to end against a live model.
Across families, tokenizer mismatch drives the divergence to a
saturated maximum, so a model swap is caught with a large margin
($D_{\mathrm{KL}}=4.1$); within a family, a black-box statistic preserves the
calibration at roughly half to three-quarters of the white-box magnitude.
An ablation of the probe-construction role (\S\ref{sec:exp:ablate}) shows that
representation alignment contributes a transferable, low-rank probe set, the
property on which black-box cross-model comparison rests; it does not maximize
the raw per-probe divergence.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Large language models are expensive to train, and their weights are the primary
carrier of that investment. This raises a natural and increasingly practical
question: given two models, can we determine whether their parameters are
(nearly) identical? The question matters for at least three reasons. First,
\emph{copy detection}: verifying whether a released checkpoint has been
surreptitiously derived from a proprietary model. Second,
\emph{provenance}: attributing a model to its training lineage. Third,
\emph{tamper auditing}: detecting whether a served model has been silently
modified, for instance, by a malicious actor injecting fine-tuned backdoors or
by an operator swapping weights at a load balancer.

Answering this question is hard for two coupled reasons. On one hand, the
weights themselves are typically not exposed: a deployment only offers token
distributions (or, at most, top-$k$ log-probabilities) through an API. On the
other, \emph{output equivalence does not imply weight equivalence}: two very
different parameterizations can realize the same input--output mapping, so
agreement on a held-out set of prompts is a necessary but weak signal.

We propose to sidestep both difficulties by exploiting the
\emph{second-order} response of the model's observable distribution: the
distributional divergence of the observable next-token distribution, whose
quadratic response is controlled by the Fisher information. A model's weights
induce a specific local geometry in representation space. If we can produce a
set of sentences that map to a tight cluster under the model's own
intermediate representation, then the way in which these
representation-aligned sentences spread in next-token probability space is a
signature of the weights themselves. Perturbing the weights perturbs this
distributional geometry in a way that a single-output comparison cannot reveal.
Throughout, ``alignment'' is defined relative to the model's own
representation: the probes must land on (nearly) the same latent
representation, and are \emph{not} required to be synonyms in any linguistic
sense. The role of this alignment is structural: the probe set stays low-rank
and concentrated when read on a different model, which is what makes the
fingerprint transferable across models.

Concretely, we make three contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Constrained representation-aligned generation}
  (\S\ref{sec:syn}): a discrete optimization procedure that, given a
  reference sentence, emits multiple sentences the model's representation places
  near it, so the probe set is low-rank and transferable across models, while
  differing on the surface, using only tokens
  from a restricted (shared) subword vocabulary. The output is always a discrete
  token sequence, never a vector. Synthesis is a one-time white-box step
  performed on a local open-weight model; detection reads only top-$k$
  log-probabilities.

  \item \textbf{Distributional-divergence fingerprinting}
  (\S\ref{sec:cov}): a fingerprint defined as the mean per-probe next-token KL
  divergence between two models, together with a two-sample change-detection
  test (mean per-probe $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ as the primary verdict, with a
  per-token $\chi^2$ aggregate plus mean-vector and covariance-spectrum
  diagnostics).

  \item \textbf{Empirical calibration and limits} (\S\ref{sec:exp}): a
  calibration curve showing that the fingerprint diverges monotonically with
  added Gaussian weight noise, consistent across model families and the two
  Qwen layers ($L8$/$L16$); a black-box top-$k$ readout that detects
  fine-tuning, weight quantization, and wholesale model swapping by a wide
  margin (\S\ref{sec:mods}); a zero-noise determinism check over the DeepSeek
  API; and a characterization of cross-family comparison in which tokenizer
  mismatch drives the fingerprint to its maximal (saturated) signal, so a
  change of model family is detected decisively, while fine-grained
  attribution remains bounded.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Discrete adversarial attacks.}
Our representation-aligned generator descends from the family of gradient-based
discrete text optimizers initiated by \citet{hotflip}, which approximates the
effect of a token substitution by the inner product of the input-embedding
gradient and the candidate embedding. Subsequent work refined this idea with
beam search and constrained candidate sets \citep{textfooler}. We differ in
purpose and in our candidate constraint. We optimize for \emph{preservation of
the model's representation}, so the output must remain near the reference in the
model's own representation, whereas HotFlip-style attacks seek a label flip. Our
candidates are also drawn from a shared subword vocabulary, which is essential
for cross-model comparison.

\paragraph{Representation similarity.}
Measuring the similarity of neural representations has a long history, with
centered kernel alignment (CKA) \citep{cka} and representational similarity
analysis \citep{rsa} as the canonical tools. These methods compare the
\emph{internal activations} of two networks and thus require white-box access.
Our fingerprint is instead computed on the observable log-probability surface,
making it compatible with API-only deployments, while still being grounded in
the intermediate representation through the probe-construction step.

\paragraph{Model fingerprinting and extraction.}
Model extraction attacks \citep{modelsteal} and watermarking
\citep{watermark} both fingerprint a model's \emph{behavior}; weight-recovery
attacks \citep{rolnick} recover the \emph{parameters} directly. Our work
occupies the middle ground: we estimate a second-order statistic that is
sensitive to the weights but does not require recovering them, and is
observable without hidden states.

\paragraph{Query-based model fingerprinting.}
The closest prior art infers a model's origin by actively probing it with
optimized queries \citep{teacherfingerprint}. We extend this line of work in
two directions. First, our probes are \emph{actively synthesized} to be
representation-aligned, placed together in the model's own intermediate
representation; the signal is therefore a within-representation-cluster
distributional divergence. Second, our statistic is the \emph{KL divergence}
between the per-probe next-token distributions, which is far more sensitive to
weight perturbation than their mean.

\section{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:form}

Let a language model $M_\theta$ be parameterized by weights $\theta \in \R^P$,
with vocabulary $\V$ and input-embedding matrix $\bm{E} \in \R^{|\V| \times D}$.
For an input token sequence $\bm{x} = (x_1, \dots, x_T) \in \V^T$, denote by
$\bm{h}^{(\ell)}(\bm{x}) \in \R^{T \times D}$ the hidden states at intermediate
layer $\ell$, and by $\bar{\bm{h}}^{(\ell)}(\bm{x}) = \frac1T
\sum_t \bm{h}^{(\ell)}_t(\bm{x}) \in \R^D$ its mean-pooled sentence vector.
The model maps a prefix to a next-token distribution
$p_\theta(\cdot \mid \bm{x}) = \softmax(\bm{W} \bm{h}^{(L)}(\bm{x}))$ over the
vocabulary.

\paragraph{Goal.}
Given black-box access to two models $M_\theta$ and $M_{\theta'}$ (top-$k$
log-probabilities only), decide whether $\norm{\theta - \theta'} \le \epsilon$
for a small $\epsilon$, i.e., whether their weights are nearly identical.

\paragraph{Two-part strategy.}
We first construct a \emph{probe set} $\mathcal{P} = \{\bm{x}^{(1)},
\dots, \bm{x}^{(m)}\}$ of representation-aligned sentences, sentences the
model's intermediate representation places near a common reference
(\S\ref{sec:syn}). Each probe
$\bm{x}^{(i)}$ induces an observable distribution $p_\theta(\cdot \mid
\bm{x}^{(i)})$; we collect a fingerprint statistic from these distributions
(\S\ref{sec:cov}). Equivalence is then tested by comparing two fingerprints
collected at different times or from different models (\S\ref{sec:detect}).

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

The fingerprinting procedure works in two roles with different access
requirements. \emph{Probe synthesis} (\S\ref{sec:syn}) is a one-time
white-box step: we construct the representation-aligned probes on a local
open-weight model, using its intermediate hidden states to enforce latent
alignment. \emph{Fingerprint readout and detection} (\S\ref{sec:cov},
\S\ref{sec:detect}) are black-box: given a candidate served model, we collect
the top-$k$ next-token log-probabilities induced by the probe set and compare
them against a reference fingerprint. Only the readout stage assumes the model
is served through an API that exposes token-level log-probabilities, specifically
the top-$k$ next-token log-probabilities at each decoding position; a provider
that withholds or quantizes them makes the readout unobservable, as noted in
\S\ref{sec:disc}. The one-time white-box synthesis step does not compromise the
black-box compatibility of the detection itself, which at query time needs only
top-$k$ log-probabilities.

\subsection{Constrained Representation-Aligned Generation}
\label{sec:syn}

We want, for a reference sentence $\bm{s}$ with latent representation
$\bm{h}^\star = \bar{\bm{h}}^{(\ell)}(\bm{s})$, a collection of sentences
$\{\bm{s}^{(j)}\}$ such that (i) each $\bm{s}^{(j)}$ is
\emph{representation-aligned} to $\bm{s}$, placed near it by the model's own
intermediate representation, and (ii) $\bm{s}^{(j)} \ne \bm{s}$ on
the surface, and (iii) every token of $\bm{s}^{(j)}$ lies in a
\emph{restricted subword vocabulary} $\Vsh \subseteq \V$.

\paragraph{Alignment as a latent constraint.}
The notion of alignment in (i) is deliberately \emph{model-relative}: two
sentences count as aligned for our purposes exactly when the model's own
intermediate representation places them in a tight cluster. We do not require,
and in general do not obtain, synonymy in the linguistic sense. The pair
\emph{``the lazy dog''} $\to$ \emph{``the lazy cat''} of \S\ref{sec:exp:syn}
is an alignment of this model-relative kind, with no semantic requirement. The
role of this requirement is structural: it yields a probe set that stays
low-rank and concentrated when read on a different model, which is what keeps
the black-box cross-model comparison of \S\ref{sec:exp:cross} well-conditioned.
It is not a claim that the probes maximize the raw divergence
(\S\ref{sec:exp:ablate}). We operationalize (i) as the
requirement that the pooled intermediate representation be close to the
reference:
\begin{equation}
  \bm{s}^{(j)} = \argmin_{\bm{x} \in (\Vsh)^T}
  \ \norm{\bar{\bm{h}}^{(\ell)}(\bm{x}) - \bm{h}^\star}^2
  \ \ \text{s.t.}\ \ \bm{x} \ne \bm{s},
  \label{eq:align}
\end{equation}
Because $\bm{x}$ ranges over a discrete set, we optimize
Eq.~\eqref{eq:align} by a discrete search whose candidate set is motivated by
first-order gradient intuition; as explained next, the search itself is
instantiated with embedding neighbors and exact re-scoring; the first-order
score is degenerate at the reference and is not used.

\paragraph{HotFlip-style search.}
A first-order Taylor expansion suggests an approximate change in the alignment
loss $\mathcal{L}(\bm{x}) = \norm{\bar{\bm{h}}^{(\ell)}(\bm{x}) - \bm{h}^\star}^2$
when token $x_i$ is replaced by token $t$,
\begin{equation}
  \Delta \mathcal{L}(i, t) \ \approx\ \bm{g}_i^\top \bm{e}_t,
  \label{eq:hotflip}
\end{equation}
where $\bm{e}_i \in \R^D$ is the input embedding of token $x_i$,
$\bm{g}_i = \nabla_{\bm{e}_i} \mathcal{L}$, and $\bm{e}_t$ is the embedding of
candidate token $t$. This gradient-guided score is, however, degenerate exactly
at the reference that anchors the search: the alignment loss is minimized by the
reference itself, so its gradient vanishes there and the first-order score of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:hotflip} is identically zero for every substitution. We therefore
treat Eq.~\eqref{eq:hotflip} as motivation only, since the search does not use
it, and \emph{instantiate} the search with a restricted candidate set,
re-scoring candidates exactly (Algorithm~\ref{alg:syn}). For each position the
candidates are the $q$ nearest \emph{embedding neighbors} of the current token
within $\Vsh$; neighbors are surface-different from the reference by
construction, so no explicit diversity term is needed. We then \emph{exactly}
re-score every candidate substitution with a forward pass, evaluating
$\mathcal{L}$ directly with no first-order approximation, and accept
the one that most reduces the alignment loss.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\fbox{\begin{minipage}{0.94\linewidth}
\smallskip
{\small\ttfamily
Algorithm~1: Embedding-neighbor representation-aligned generation.\par\smallskip
\hangindent=1.5em \textbf{Input:} reference $\bm{s}$, layer $\ell$, shared
vocab $\Vsh$, neighbor count $q$.\par
\hangindent=1.5em $\bm{h}^\star \gets \bar{\bm{h}}^{(\ell)}(\bm{s})$\par
\hangindent=1.5em $\mathcal{C} \gets \bigcup_i \big\{ (i,t) : t \in \Vsh,\
t \ne s_i,\ t \in \mathrm{NN}_q(s_i) \big\}$\par
\hangindent=1.5em $\bm{x}^\star \gets \argmin_{(i,t) \in \mathcal{C}}\
\norm{\bar{\bm{h}}^{(\ell)}(\bm{x}_{i\to t}) - \bm{h}^\star}^2$\par
\hangindent=1.5em \quad ($\bm{x}_{i\to t}$ denotes $\bm{s}$ with token $i$
replaced by $t$)\par
\hangindent=1.5em \textbf{return} $\bm{x}^\star$ if it improves over $\bm{s}$,
else $\bm{s}$\par
}
\smallskip
\end{minipage}}
\caption{Embedding-neighbor representation-aligned generation.}
\label{alg:syn}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Distributional-Divergence Fingerprint}
\label{sec:cov}

Given a probe set $\mathcal{P} = \{\bm{x}^{(1)}, \dots, \bm{x}^{(m)}\}$, each
probe induces a next-token distribution over the vocabulary, which we represent
by its log-probability vector restricted to the shared vocabulary $\Vsh$:
\begin{equation}
  \bm{\phi}_\theta(\bm{x}) = \big[\ \log p_\theta(t \mid \bm{x}) \big]_{t \in \Vsh}
  \in \R^{|\Vsh|},
  \label{eq:phi}
\end{equation}
so that $\bm{\phi}_\theta$ is always a vector over $\Vsh$. In the white-box
(local) setting $\Vsh = \V$ and $\bm{\phi}$ is available for the full
vocabulary, so $p_\theta(\cdot \mid \bm{x}) = \exp \bm{\phi}_\theta(\bm{x})$ is
a genuine probability distribution over $\Vsh$. In the black-box (API) setting
only the top-$k$ entries are returned; we form the \emph{top-$k$ truncated KL}
by keeping the observed top-$k$ entries and collecting the unobserved tail mass
into a single renormalized bucket. (An alternative, setting the unobserved
remainder to a fixed floor $f$ without renormalizing, is the convention used in
the saturation analysis of \S\ref{sec:theory}; the two differ at every
perturbation scale.) Because the next-token probability
mass concentrates in the top-$k$, the truncated statistic tracks the
full-vocabulary KL in shape but at reduced magnitude, capturing roughly half
to three-quarters of it in our calibration (\S\ref{sec:exp:calib}). It is a
faithful detector of weight changes. To reduce sampling variance
we average $\bm{\phi}$ over $S$ independent draws per probe, yielding per-probe
means $\bar{\bm{\phi}}^{(i)}$.

We define the \emph{fingerprint} of $M_\theta$ as the collection of per-probe
next-token distributions $\{\bar{\bm{\phi}}^{(1)}, \dots,
\bar{\bm{\phi}}^{(m)}\}$, and measure the difference between two models
$\theta$ and $\theta'$ by the mean per-probe KL divergence
\begin{equation}
  D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\theta, \theta')
  \;=\; \frac1m \sum_{i=1}^m
    \KL\!\big(p_\theta(\cdot \mid \bm{x}^{(i)})\ \|\ p_{\theta'}(\cdot \mid \bm{x}^{(i)})\big),
  \label{eq:dkl}
\end{equation}
computed over the \emph{union} of the two models' observed top-$k$ entries plus
a single renormalized tail bucket, so no token in either model's top-$k$ falls
in the other model's tail bucket. In the black-box setting this is the
truncated KL divergence of the preceding paragraph.
The KL divergence is the central object: it measures how far the next-token
distributions induced by sentences that are \emph{representation-aligned},
placed together by the model itself, have moved under a change of
parameters, a quantity that is a direct functional of the weights but is not
exposed by any single forward pass.

\subsection{Theoretical Analysis}
\label{sec:theory}


\paragraph{Quadratic response and the Fisher information.}
For a fixed probe $\bm{x}$, the next-token log-probability vector
$\bm{\phi}_\theta(\bm{x})$ is a smooth function of the weights, with Jacobian
$\bm{J}_\theta(\bm{x}) = \frac{\partial \bm{\phi}_\theta(\bm{x})}{\partial
\theta}$. Its rows are score functions (Proposition~\ref{prop:score}, proved
in \S\ref{sec:theory-app}), so the mean per-probe KL divergence between
$\theta$ and $\theta + \delta$ admits the second-order expansion
\begin{equation}
  D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\theta, \theta+\delta)
  = \tfrac12\, \delta^\top \bar{\bm{F}}_\theta\, \delta
    + O(\norm{\delta}_2^3),
  \qquad
  \bar{\bm{F}}_\theta = \frac1m \sum_{i=1}^m
    \bm{J}_\theta(\bm{x}^{(i)})^\top
    \operatorname{diag}\!\big(p_\theta(\cdot \mid \bm{x}^{(i)})\big)
    \bm{J}_\theta(\bm{x}^{(i)})
  \;\succeq\; 0,
  \label{eq:dklexpand}
\end{equation}
where the first-order term vanishes and $\bar{\bm{F}}_\theta$ is the
probe-averaged Fisher information, a probability-weighted squared Jacobian
(Proposition~\ref{prop:fisher}, \S\ref{sec:theory-app}). Because
$\norm{\delta}_2 \propto r$ under the random-perturbation protocol of
\S\ref{sec:exp:calib}, the divergence grows quadratically in $r$ for small
perturbations and is monotone along any fixed direction.

\paragraph{Saturation.}
The quadratic regime is bounded: for an arbitrarily large $\delta$ the
divergence does not grow without bound but rises to a stable ceiling set by the
clean model's entropy and the log-probability floor
(Proposition~\ref{prop:saturation}, \S\ref{sec:theory-app}). The perturbation
response therefore passes through three regimes
(Table~\ref{tab:regimes}), from a quadratic (Fisher-information) regime at
small $\delta$, through a polynomial regime, to a saturated ceiling at large
$\delta$. The fingerprint is a faithful, monotone proxy for weight change
within the first two regimes, which is the operating range the detector is
called upon to police.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Regimes of the fingerprint response to weight perturbation.}
\label{tab:regimes}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Regime & Response & Mechanism \\
\midrule
Small (sufficiently small $\norm{\delta}_2$) & $D_{\mathrm{KL}} \approx c\, r^2$ (quadratic) & Fisher information, Prop.~\ref{prop:fisher} \\
Moderate & $D_{\mathrm{KL}} \approx c\, r^2 + c' r^3 + \cdots$ & higher-order terms, Prop.~\ref{prop:fisher} \\
Large & $D_{\mathrm{KL}} \to -H(p_\theta) - \log f$ (saturation) & disjoint top-$k$ support + floor, Prop.~\ref{prop:saturation} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Adversarial invariance.}
An adversary who knows the entire method can in principle engineer a targeted
weight edit $\delta$ that leaves the fingerprint invariant to second order, but
such an edit must lie in the null space of the stacked probe Jacobian
(Proposition~\ref{prop:stealth}), a measure-zero subspace, and any edit with a
nonzero visible component forces a global compensation whose norm is bounded
below by Eq.~\eqref{eq:compcost}. The analysis, collected in
\S\ref{sec:theory-app}, shows that these costs make successful evasion
computationally prohibitive for the models considered, even though it is not
ruled out in principle (Remark~\ref{rem:adv}).
\subsection{Change Detection}
\label{sec:detect}

Given a reference collection of per-probe next-token distributions from
$M_{\theta_0}$ and a re-probe collected from a candidate model, we combine the
distributional-divergence statistic with a per-token test into a verdict.

\paragraph{Per-probe KL divergence.}
We estimate the per-probe next-token distributions from the observed
log-probabilities (top-$k$ plus floor), and evaluate the mean per-probe KL
divergence $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:dkl}, which by the analysis of
\S\ref{sec:theory} grows quadratically in any weight perturbation and is
exactly zero in the no-change regime.

\paragraph{Per-token two-sample test.}
For each token $t$ observed with real data in both collections, we form the
two-sample $z$-score
\begin{equation}
  z_t = \frac{\mu_{1,t} - \mu_{0,t}}{\sqrt{s_{0,t}^2/n_0 + s_{1,t}^2/n_1}},
  \label{eq:z}
\end{equation}
where $\mu_{j,t}$ and $s_{j,t}^2$ are the sample mean and variance of token
$t$'s log-probability across the probe set in collection $j$. When the two
collections are identical (a deterministic API, as in \S\ref{sec:exp:api}), the
\emph{numerator} $\mu_{1,t}-\mu_{0,t}$ vanishes while the across-probe variances
remain nonzero, so $z_t = 0$; when both numerator and denominator vanish (a
token constant across all probes) we set $z_t := 0$ by convention. (A nonzero
numerator with a vanishing denominator, a token constant within each collection
but at different values, does not arise for a deterministic readout and is
excluded.) Summing the
$z_t^2$ into a $\chi^2$ statistic with the number of
compared tokens as degrees of freedom must be read as a \emph{heuristic}
aggregate: the per-token scores from a
single softmax are correlated (a shift in one token's log-probability moves the
others), so the sum-of-squares overstates the evidence and should be treated as
an upper-bound-style diagnostic. A principled alternative would be a
multivariate Hotelling $T^2$ / Mahalanobis statistic on the joint log-probability
vector, which we leave to future work; the change verdict rests primarily on
$D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:dkl}.

\paragraph{Mean-vector and covariance-spectrum diagnostics.}
On the common-token subset, we also report how the distributions moved, using
the mean vector $\bm{\mu}_j = \tfrac1m \sum_{i=1}^m \bar{\bm{\phi}}^{(i)}_j$ of
the per-probe log-probability vectors (collection $j \in \{0,1\}$),
\begin{equation}
  D_{\mathrm{mean}} = \frac{\norm{\bm{\mu}_1 - \bm{\mu}_0}}{\norm{\bm{\mu}_0}} ,
  \label{eq:dmean}
\end{equation}
and their covariance spectra $\bm{\lambda}_0, \bm{\lambda}_1$,
\begin{equation}
  D_{\mathrm{cov}} = \frac{\norm{\bm{\lambda}_1 - \bm{\lambda}_0}}{\norm{\bm{\lambda}_0}} .
  \label{eq:dcov}
\end{equation}
These are diagnostic only: $D_{\mathrm{mean}}$ is sensitive but weak, and
$D_{\mathrm{cov}}$ can saturate or even fall at large perturbations, so neither
serves as the primary statistic (\S\ref{sec:exp:calib}).

A model is declared \textsc{changed} if the primary statistic, the mean
per-probe KL divergence $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$, exceeds a threshold set by its
monotone calibration to $r$ (\S\ref{sec:exp:calib}); the per-token $\chi^2$
aggregate of Eq.~\eqref{eq:z} is reported as a supporting diagnostic only,
subject to the independence caveats noted above. In the zero-noise regime the
re-probe reproduces the reference distributions exactly, so $D_{\mathrm{KL}} =
0$, every $z_t = 0$ by convention, and the detector admits no false positives
(\S\ref{sec:exp}).

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:exp}

\paragraph{Setup.}
Local calibration experiments use three small open-weight models:
\texttt{Qwen\slash Qwen3.5-0.8B} ($|\V|=248{,}077$ subword tokens),
\texttt{unsloth\slash Llama-3.2-1B} ($|\V|=128{,}256$), and
\texttt{unsloth\slash gemma-3-1b-pt} ($|\V|=262{,}145$), at intermediate
layer $8$ (and layer $16$ for Qwen) with mean pooling; the black-box
experiments use the DeepSeek API (\texttt{deepseek-v4-flash}, $|\V|=129{,}280$)
which exposes
top-$20$ log-probabilities with reasoning disabled. The shared subword
vocabulary with DeepSeek contains $108{,}773$ tokens for Qwen ($43.8\%$ of its
vocabulary), $70{,}663$ for Llama, and only $21{,}237$ for Gemma ($8.1\%$ of
its much larger vocabulary), so the \emph{surface} overlap varies
substantially across families. As \S\ref{sec:exp:cross} shows, however, even a
large surface overlap does not translate into prediction overlap.

\paragraph{Hyperparameters and reproducibility.}
Probe generation uses three reference sentences, ``The quick brown fox jumps
over the lazy dog.'', ``The committee approved the new budget proposal this
morning.'', and ``She walked slowly through the old town under the rain.'',
and, per reference, an embedding-neighbor candidate set with
$k_{\mathrm{neighbors}} = 200$ (the neighbor count $q$ of
Algorithm~\ref{alg:syn}), an inner top-$k$ of $6$ candidates retained per
position, and $M = 20$ candidate probes. The observed token set for the
per-token diagnostics is the top-$20$
next-token log-probability surface (matching the API); locally (white-box), the
KL divergence of Table~\ref{tab:calib} is evaluated over the full vocabulary with
log-probabilities clamped at $-30$ to avoid underflow, and the black-box
top-$20$ truncated variant is characterized separately in
\S\ref{sec:exp:calib}. Calibration
injects a single fixed Gaussian direction (seed $0$) rescaled by
$r \in \{0, 0.02, 0.05, 0.10, 0.20, 0.40, 0.60, 1.00\}$, at intermediate layers
$8$ and $16$ (only Qwen was run at $L16$; Llama and Gemma at $L8$). The API
experiment uses $15$ probe contexts ($3$ references $\times$ $5$ surface
variants, drawn from the $M = 20$ candidates) with $2$ samples each.

\subsection{Probe Quality and the Limits of Alignment}
\label{sec:exp:syn}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.48\linewidth]{figures/fig_discriminative.pdf}
\caption{Discriminative power of hidden-state cosine similarity, by layer.
Representation-aligned (alg) pairs and unrelated (rnd) pairs are compared;
the annotated gap (mean cosine difference) peaks at layer $16$.}
\label{fig:disc}
\end{figure}

Intermediate-layer alignment alone is a \emph{necessary but not sufficient}
condition for representation alignment: Sequences that align closely with $\bm{h}^\star$ are
not always fluent or semantically coherent; the latent-cluster objective
admits degenerate solutions; with an unconstrained candidate set the search collapses to
the reference itself unchanged. Quantitatively, hidden-state cosine similarity
carries only a weak discriminative signal: across layers, the cosine gap between
representation-aligned (alg) and unrelated (rnd) sentence pairs is
$0.18$--$0.32$, with aligned pairs at cosine $\approx0.85$--$0.95$ and
unrelated pairs at $\approx0.60$--$0.73$ (Figure~\ref{fig:disc}). Restricting
the candidate set to embedding-space neighbors within $\Vsh$
(\S\ref{sec:syn}) resolves the fluency problem practically, yielding
representation-aligned pairs such as \emph{``the lazy dog'' $\to$ ``the lazy
cat''} while preserving alignment (mean cosine $\approx0.98$). This pair is
\emph{not} a synonym pair in the linguistic sense; the two sentences differ in
meaning to a human reader, yet the model's representation places them in a
tight cluster, which is exactly the notion of alignment this paper relies on.

\subsection{Calibration under Gaussian Weight Noise}
\label{sec:exp:calib}

