\subsection{Zero-Noise Change Detection over the API}
\label{sec:exp:api}

This experiment verifies that the readout pipeline is deterministic end to end
against a live API. We collect a reference distributional fingerprint over a
probe set of $15$ representation-aligned contexts (two samples each) served by
the DeepSeek API, then re-probe the same contexts. Under fixed parameters the
API is deterministic, so the re-probe reproduces the reference statistics
exactly: the per-token $\chi^2$ test is null ($\chi^2 = 0.00$, $df = 28$,
$p = 1$) and the mean per-probe KL divergence is $D_{\mathrm{KL}} = 0.000$. The
\textsc{unchanged} verdict and the absence of false positives follow
necessarily from a deterministic re-probe. They are Type-I error control and
carry no information about sensitivity. The positive case that the fingerprint
responds monotonically to weight perturbation comes from the local calibration
of \S\ref{sec:exp:calib} (Table~\ref{tab:calib}) and the local positive control
below, which perturb a fixed model, in contrast to re-probing an identical one.
Applying the same positive control to a perturbed model served through a
production API is a practical deployment step left to future work
(\S\ref{sec:disc}).

The re-probe does fix two properties of the readout that a local run leaves
open. First, the recorded log-probability field is highly concentrated: it has
trace $\operatorname{tr}(\bm{\Sigma}) = 516.8$ over $60$ observed tokens (rank
$14$), with the top three eigenvalues accounting for $73\%$ of the total
variance, a low-rank structure (Figure~\ref{fig:spectrum}) that reflects the
fact that the probes collapse onto a single representation-alignment cluster.
This spectral concentration characterizes the probe set; the change verdict
rests on the divergence. Second, the pipeline from the top-$k$ readout to the
$\chi^2$ and KL statistics runs unchanged against a real served model.

\paragraph{Local positive control.}
To supply the positive case missing from the API experiment, we run the
change-detection test of \S\ref{sec:detect} on a \emph{locally} perturbed model:
Qwen-0.8B clean versus $N(0,(r\sigma)^2)$-perturbed, over the same probe set
($\nu = 209$ compared tokens, the union of the top-$20$ next-tokens over the
probes). At $r = 0$ the test is null ($\chi^2 = 0$, $D_{\mathrm{KL}} = 0$); at
$r = 0.10$ it fires decisively ($\chi^2 = 1460$ versus a null expectation of
$\nu = 209$; $D_{\mathrm{KL}} = 0.61$ full-vocabulary, or $0.40$ top-$20$
truncated, the statistic a black-box detector would actually observe). The
per-token $\chi^2$ is, however, insensitive at small perturbations: at
$r \le 0.05$ it reports $p \approx 1$ even though $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ is already
$0.11$, because the perturbation inflates the across-probe variance in the
$z$-score denominator of Eq.~\eqref{eq:z} at least as fast as it shifts the
means, so the observed $\chi^2$ ($127$ at $r = 0.05$) sits \emph{below} its null
expectation $\nu = 209$, and the test gains power only once
the mean shift outgrows the variance inflation (by $r = 0.10$). This confirms $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ as
the primary verdict, with the $\chi^2$ aggregate supporting it (\S\ref{sec:detect}). The
full-vocabulary $D_{\mathrm{KL}} = 0.61$ uses the same $-30$ clamp as
Table~\ref{tab:calib} and is consistent with its $5$-seed spread
($0.57 \pm 0.23$ at $r = 0.10$).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.48\linewidth]{figures/fig_spectrum.pdf}
\caption{Spectrum of the per-probe log-probability covariance of the DeepSeek
fingerprint (log scale). The rapid decay of eigenvalues, with the top three
accounting for $73\%$ of the trace, reflects the concentration of
representation-aligned probes onto a single cluster.}
\label{fig:spectrum}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Comparison}
\label{sec:exp:cross}

The local open-weight models of \S\ref{sec:exp:calib} serve \emph{only} to
generate the probes: the fingerprint readout is black-box, so a cross-family
comparison reads the induced top-$k$ log-probabilities from whichever model is
queried. Cross-family comparison is, in fact, the regime in which the
fingerprint is \emph{most} decisive. Two mechanisms conspire to push the
divergence onto its maximal ceiling. First, \emph{surface tokenizer overlap} is
small: although the Qwen and DeepSeek families share $108{,}773$ subword
tokens, that shared vocabulary is a small fraction of each family's full
vocabulary, and surface-shared tokens need not be the ones that carry
predictive mass. Second, and more importantly, \emph{model-difference
saturation}: on a shared context, the top-$20$ next-tokens predicted by the two
models have an average Jaccard overlap of only $0.012$ (on average $0.7$ shared
tokens, out of roughly $39$ and $17$ predicted tokens respectively, the union
of the top-$20$ next-tokens over the probe set, for Qwen and for DeepSeek). The
representation-aligned probes therefore induce log-probability vectors whose
supports are almost disjoint across the two models. This disjoint-support regime
is exactly the large-$\delta$ saturation of Proposition~\ref{prop:saturation},
realized here by a change of model family. It is the strongest form of detection
the fingerprint offers: the per-probe divergence rises to its maximal,
model-dependent ceiling, so \emph{any} distinct model family is
caught with an unmistakably large margin. The point is quantified in
\S\ref{sec:mods}: a wholesale Qwen$\to$Llama swap registers $D_{\mathrm{KL}} =
4.41$ (full) $/\,4.11$ (top-$20$), more than an order of magnitude above a
supervised fine-tune ($0.17$) and far beyond any calibration threshold. The
very disjointness that frustrates \emph{within}-family calibration is, across
families, the detector's greatest asset. The representation-aligned probes
retain their low-rank concentration when read across model families
(\S\ref{sec:exp:ablate}), so this cross-family readout rests on a
well-conditioned signal.

What saturation forecloses is not \emph{detection} but \emph{calibrated
attribution}. Once the observed supports are disjoint, the divergence leaves the
Fisher-quadratic regime of \S\ref{sec:theory}: it can no longer be inverted to a
quantitative perturbation scale $r$, and a sufficiently large perturbation of
the reference is asymptotically indistinguishable from a different model
(Proposition~\ref{prop:saturation}). We can therefore assert with confidence
that the served model is \emph{not} the reference, but not, from the divergence
alone, precisely \emph{which} model it is or \emph{how far} its weights differ.
Cross-family comparison is thus the clearest instantiation of change detection,
while remaining a known bound on fine-grained attribution, distinct
from the \emph{within}-family setting in which the model is fixed and only its
weights are perturbed.

\section{Detection of Fine-Tuning, Model Swapping, Weight Quantization, and RL Alignment}
\label{sec:mods}

The calibration of \S\ref{sec:exp:calib} measures the response of the
distributional-divergence fingerprint to injected Gaussian weight noise on a
fixed model. Two natural adversarial settings, a model that has been
\emph{fine-tuned} (e.g.\ by LoRA) and a model that has been \emph{swapped} for a
different open-weight model, were tested directly on a production GPU
(NVIDIA RTX 3080 Ti) using \texttt{Qwen/Qwen3.5-0.8B} at layer $8$, with the
same $63$-probe set and shared-subword-vocabulary machinery as the rest of the
paper ($|\Vsh|=108{,}773$).

\paragraph{Fine-tuning (LoRA).}
We fine-tuned the clean Qwen checkpoint with LoRA ($r=16$, $\alpha=32$, applied
to the attention and MLP projections) on $2{,}500$ examples of
\texttt{yahma/alpaca-cleaned}, training $250$ steps of causal-LM on the
instruction--response text (loss $3.26 \to 1.05$). To measure \emph{sensitivity}
rather than a single point, we treated the trained adapter's low-rank update
$\Delta W$ as a fixed direction and read the fingerprint at interpolated scales
$W_s = W_0 + s\,\Delta W$ for $s \in \{0.05,\dots,1.0\}$.
Table~\ref{tab:lora} reports the mean per-probe KL divergence from the clean
model.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{LoRA fine-tuning sensitivity on Qwen-0.8B (L8): mean per-probe
$D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ between the clean model and the model with the LoRA adapter
at interpolation scale $s$ ($W_s = W_0 + s\,\Delta W$), and the equivalent
Gaussian-noise scale $r_{\mathrm{eq}}$ obtained by inverting the
\S\ref{sec:exp:calib} calibration.}
\label{tab:lora}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
$s$ & $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ (full) & $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ (top-20) & $r_{\mathrm{eq}}$ \\
\midrule
0.00 & 0.0000 & 0.0000 & 0.000 \\
0.05 & 0.0010 & 0.0008 & 0.001 \\
0.10 & 0.0022 & 0.0018 & 0.003 \\
0.20 & 0.0075 & 0.0061 & 0.009 \\
0.40 & 0.0284 & 0.0234 & 0.024 \\
0.70 & 0.0872 & 0.0735 & 0.046 \\
1.00 & 0.1734 & 0.1477 & 0.059 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The response is monotone and, within the small-scale regime, approximately
\emph{quadratic} in $s$ ($D_{\mathrm{KL}} \approx 0.174\,s^2$), exactly the
Fisher-information behavior predicted by Proposition~\ref{prop:fisher} for a
\emph{structured}, low-rank weight edit as well as for isotropic Gaussian
noise. A full 250-step LoRA fine-tune is detected at
$D_{\mathrm{KL}} = 0.173$, equivalent to Gaussian noise of scale
$r_{\mathrm{eq}} \approx 0.06$, decisively above the smallest calibrated scale
($r=0.02$, $D_{\mathrm{KL}}=0.016$); the fingerprint crosses that detection
floor already at $s \approx 0.3$ (about $30\%$ of the trained adapter's
strength). The black-box top-20 truncated statistic retains roughly $85\%$ of
the full-vocabulary magnitude and remains monotone. Because the LoRA update is
a fixed low-rank direction, the quadratic response in $s$ follows from the same
second-order analysis as the noise calibration, confirming that distributional
divergence detects fine-tuning with the same quadratic sensitivity as it
detects arbitrary weight perturbation.

\paragraph{Model swapping (Qwen $\to$ Llama).}
We then tested the extreme case in which the served model is not a perturbed
Qwen but a \emph{different} open-weight model, \texttt{unsloth/Llama-3.2-1B}.
Because the two tokenizers differ, the probe set was re-tokenized on the Llama
tokenizer and the fingerprint read over the $115{,}771$ subword tokens shared
\emph{by surface} between the two vocabularies. The resulting mean per-probe KL
is $D_{\mathrm{KL}} = 4.41$ (full) $/\,4.11$ (top-20), more than an
\emph{order of magnitude} above the LoRA fine-tune ($0.17$) and above any
reasonable fine-tuning signal. The two models' next-token supports are almost
disjoint, so the comparison sits in the saturation regime of
Proposition~\ref{prop:saturation}, exactly as predicted.
Figure~\ref{fig:lora} summarizes both results. We conclude that distributional
divergence \emph{reliably and sensitively} detects fine-tuning, and detects a
wholesale model swap with an overwhelmingly large margin.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\linewidth]{figures/fig_lora_sensitivity.pdf}
\caption{Fingerprint sensitivity to fine-tuning and model swapping.
(a)~Mean per-probe $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ of a Qwen-0.8B checkpoint LoRA-tuned on
alpaca-cleaned, vs.\ adapter scale $s$; the response is quadratic
($\approx 0.17\,s^2$) and crosses the $r{=}0.02$ detection floor at
$s \approx 0.3$. The dashed red line marks the Qwen$\to$Llama swap,
$\sim 25\times$ larger. (b)~The same LoRA divergence mapped through the
\S\ref{sec:exp:calib} calibration to an equivalent Gaussian-noise scale
$r_{\mathrm{eq}}$, reaching $\approx 0.06$ at the full adapter.}
\label{fig:lora}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Weight-only quantization.}
A further and increasingly common service-side modification is \emph{lossy
weight quantization}: an operator serves a quantized (e.g.\ 4-bit or 8-bit)
copy of a checkpoint to cut memory and latency, so the deployed model is not
exactly the reference weights. Because quantization is a deterministic,
structured, and comparatively small-norm edit, unlike the Gaussian noise of
\S\ref{sec:exp:calib} or the low-rank drift of a LoRA adapter, it is a distinct
stress test for the fingerprint. We implemented weight-only round-to-nearest
(RTN) quantization with per-row absmax scaling on the Qwen-0.8B checkpoint
(layer $8$, the same $63$-probe set and shared-subword-vocabulary machinery as
the rest of the paper), quantizing all transformer linear layers to $k$ bits
while keeping the input embeddings and the output projection in bf16, matching
the configuration of typical quantized serving.
Table~\ref{tab:quant} reports the mean per-probe $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ from the
clean model as a function of the bit width $k$.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Weight-only RTN quantization sensitivity on Qwen-0.8B (L8): mean
per-probe $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ between the clean model and the $k$-bit quantized
model, and the equivalent Gaussian-noise scale $r_{\mathrm{eq}}$ obtained by
inverting the \S\ref{sec:exp:calib} calibration.}
\label{tab:quant}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
$k$ & $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ (full) & $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ (top-20) & $r_{\mathrm{eq}}$ \\
\midrule
8 & 0.0021 & 0.0013 & 0.003 \\
6 & 0.0205 & 0.0110 & 0.021 \\
5 & 0.1116 & 0.0743 & 0.051 \\
4 & 0.8221 & 0.5787 & 0.111 \\
3 & 5.9032 & 4.5438 & 0.289 \\
2 & 10.2025 & 8.3185 & 0.454 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The response is strictly monotone in the quantization strength: 8-bit
weight-only quantization, whose per-tensor relative $\ell_2$ error is on the
order of $10^{-3}$, is essentially \emph{invisible} to the fingerprint
($D_{\mathrm{KL}} = 0.0021$, $r_{\mathrm{eq}} \approx 0.003$), an order of
magnitude below the smallest calibrated scale ($r=0.02$,
$D_{\mathrm{KL}}=0.016$). As the bit width drops below $6$, however, the signal
emerges abruptly and decisively: 6-bit already reaches the detection floor
($0.020$); 4-bit, the configuration most often chosen for memory-constrained
serving, is detected at $D_{\mathrm{KL}}=0.82$ ($r_{\mathrm{eq}} \approx 0.11$),
an order of magnitude above the floor; and 2-bit is detected at
$D_{\mathrm{KL}}=10.2$ ($r_{\mathrm{eq}} \approx 0.45$). The black-box top-20
truncated statistic retains roughly $70$--$85\%$ of the full-vocabulary
magnitude and remains monotone (Table~\ref{tab:quant}), so a black-box detector
observes the same verdict. This abrupt threshold reflects that quantization
error is a structured, non-isotropic rounding whose step
size $2^{-(k-1)}$ grows geometrically; the fingerprint therefore delineates
exactly the operating range in which a quantized served model diverges from the
reference weights.

Because RTN quantization acts on a well-defined subset of parameters, we can
probe its sensitivity continuously by quantizing only the first $f$ fraction of
transformer layers to 4 bits and leaving the rest in bf16. The mean per-probe
$D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ grows monotonically from $0.36$ ($f=0.10$) to $0.82$
($f=1.00$), with the detection floor crossed already at $f=0.10$, so even a
partially quantized model is detected, and the fingerprint tracks the extent of
compression (Figure~\ref{fig:quant}).

The same int4/int8 probe is consistent across model families: for
Llama-3.2-1B and Gemma-3-1b, each compared against its \emph{own} clean
fingerprint on the same shared-vocabulary probe set, 4-bit quantization is
detected at $D_{\mathrm{KL}} = 0.62$ and $0.37$ respectively
($r_{\mathrm{eq}} \approx 0.15$ and $0.06$), while 8-bit remains below the
detection floor ($0.003$ and $0.002$) (Figure~\ref{fig:quant}). The qualitative
regime, int4 detected and int8 invisible, thus holds across all three open-weight
families of this paper, despite their differing vocabularies and scales.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\linewidth]{figures/fig_quant_sensitivity.pdf}
\caption{Fingerprint sensitivity to weight-only quantization.
(a)~Mean per-probe $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ of Qwen-0.8B (L8) vs.\ quantization bit
width $k$ (full-vocabulary and top-20 truncated); the response is strictly
monotone, reaching the detection floor between 6 and 8 bits. Cross-model-family
int4/int8 points for Llama and Gemma are shown as diamonds. (b)~The same
divergence as a function of the fraction $f$ of layers quantized to 4 bits, a
continuous monotone curve crossing the $r{=}0.02$ floor already at $f\approx
0.1$.}
\label{fig:quant}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Quantization as a boundary of detectability.}
The 8-bit case is informative: because the fingerprint measures a second-order
response that scales with the \emph{squared} visible weight change, a
sufficiently small-norm structured edit, of which 8-bit weight-only rounding is
a canonical example, falls below the detection floor of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:floor} and is indistinguishable from the clean weights. This
delineates precisely the operating range the fingerprint is called upon to
police: within it the detector is a faithful, monotone proxy for weight change,
with sensitivity bounded below, so high-bit-width lossy compression can evade
it, while the configurations chosen for memory- or latency-constrained serving
(4-bit and below) are detected decisively.

\paragraph{Reinforcement-learning alignment (DPO).}
A distinct and increasingly dominant way a served model diverges from the
reference weights is \emph{preference-based alignment}: instead of supervised
fine-tuning on input--output pairs, the model is optimized to prefer
high-quality responses (RLHF/DPO). To test whether the fingerprint detects such
an RL update, and how its sensitivity compares with the supervised LoRA
fine-tune above, we trained a LoRA adapter ($r=16$, the same target modules) with
\emph{direct preference optimization} (DPO) on $1{,}000$ preference pairs from a
\texttt{Dahoas/rm-static} subset, using the DPO loss
$\mathcal{L}=-\log\sigma\big(\beta[(\log p_\theta(y^+)-\log p_\theta(y^-))-(\log
p_{\mathrm{ref}}(y^+)-\log p_{\mathrm{ref}}(y^-))]\big)$ with $\beta=0.1$ and the
frozen clean Qwen model as the reference. As before we treated the trained
adapter's low-rank update $\Delta W$ as a fixed direction and read the fingerprint
at interpolated scales $W_s = W_0 + s\,\Delta W$. Table~\ref{tab:rl} and
Figure~\ref{fig:rl} report the result.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{DPO (RL) alignment sensitivity on Qwen-0.8B (L8): mean per-probe
$D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ between the clean model and the model with the LoRA adapter
trained by direct preference optimization, at interpolation scale $s$ ($W_s =
W_0 + s\,\Delta W$), and the equivalent Gaussian-noise scale
$r_{\mathrm{eq}}$.}
\label{tab:rl}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
$s$ & $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ (full) & $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ (top-20) & $r_{\mathrm{eq}}$ \\
\midrule
0.00 & 0.0000 & 0.0000 & 0.000 \\
0.05 & 0.0010 & 0.0007 & 0.001 \\
0.10 & 0.0014 & 0.0009 & 0.002 \\
0.20 & 0.0037 & 0.0023 & 0.005 \\
0.40 & 0.0114 & 0.0066 & 0.014 \\
0.70 & 0.0344 & 0.0207 & 0.027 \\
1.00 & 0.0701 & 0.0442 & 0.039 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The RL adapter is \emph{detected}: $D_{\mathrm{KL}} = 0.070$ at the full
adapter, $r_{\mathrm{eq}} \approx 0.04$, above the $r{=}0.02$ detection floor
and crossed already at $s \approx 0.47$, but with a notably \emph{smaller}
signature than the supervised LoRA fine-tune above ($0.173$,
$r_{\mathrm{eq}} \approx 0.06$). This is consistent with DPO being a
\emph{KL-regularized} objective that keeps the policy close to the reference: the
resulting weight edit is smaller-norm and behaviorally more conservative than an
unregularized SFT, so the induced next-token divergence is roughly $40\%$ of the
SFT magnitude at comparable LoRA rank and training steps. The black-box top-20
truncated statistic retains roughly $60\%$ of the full-vocabulary magnitude and
remains monotone (Table~\ref{tab:rl}). RL-based alignment therefore occupies a
middle ground in this paper: a genuine, detectable weight change whose
fingerprint footprint is smaller than supervised fine-tuning, yet still within
the quadratic (Fisher-information) regime; a model swap, in contrast, reaches
the saturation regime.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\linewidth]{figures/fig_rl_sensitivity.pdf}
\caption{Fingerprint sensitivity to RL (DPO) versus supervised (LoRA)
fine-tuning on Qwen-0.8B (L8).
(a)~Mean per-probe $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ vs.\ adapter scale $s$; both are monotone
and cross the $r{=}0.02$ floor, but the DPO adapter is detected at only
$\approx40\%$ of the SFT magnitude ($0.070$ vs.\ $0.173$ at $s=1$), consistent
with DPO's KL regularization toward the reference. (b)~The same divergences
mapped through the \S\ref{sec:exp:calib} calibration to equivalent Gaussian-noise
scales $r_{\mathrm{eq}}$ ($\approx 0.04$ for DPO vs.\ $\approx 0.06$ for SFT).}
\label{fig:rl}
\end{figure}

\section{Discussion and Limitations}
\label{sec:disc}

\paragraph{What the distributional divergence buys over the mean and covariance.}
The mean log-probability vector is a first-order summary that is dominated by
the model's coarse language statistics and is relatively insensitive to the
precise weights. The covariance of the per-probe log-probability vectors is
more sensitive, but its response is dominated by the spectrum and can saturate
or collapse at large perturbations. The per-probe distributional divergence
(KL) combines the best of both: it is far more sensitive than the mean, since its
quadratic response is governed by the Fisher information, which accumulates
probability-weighted squared Jacobians; and it
remains monotone where the covariance saturates, because the KL response is a
positive quadratic form for small perturbations and a finite, entropy-governed
ceiling for large ones.

The black-box compatibility of the fingerprint is scoped as follows: probe
synthesis requires white-box access to a local open-weight model, once and
offline; the fingerprint readout and the change verdict require only the top-$k$
next-token log-probabilities of the served model at query time. The detector
itself never needs hidden states.

\paragraph{Same-model detection across users.}
The framework directly supports a natural extension: whether two parties are
served by the same underlying model. Two collections of per-probe next-token
distributions, one attributed to each party, are indistinguishable exactly when
the two models induce identical distributions on the probe set, which is the
equality decision $D_{\mathrm{KL}} \approx 0$ of \S\ref{sec:detect}. The zero-noise check of
\S\ref{sec:exp:api} is this same test specialized to re-probing one endpoint;
here the two collections come from two endpoints or two users instead. The three
readings of ``same model'' line up with the regimes of
Table~\ref{tab:regimes}: identical weights give $D_{\mathrm{KL}} = 0$, a shared
base family (two independent fine-tunes of one checkpoint) gives a small nonzero
divergence within the quadratic regime ($0.07$--$0.17$ for the adapters of
\S\ref{sec:mods}), and a different model saturates at the ceiling ($4.11$,
top-20, for the Qwen-to-Llama swap). A provider that controls routing can
therefore detect account or key sharing by injecting the probe set into each
session and comparing the two readouts, while treating the verdict as an
equality test whose threshold must be calibrated against the probe count and
the readout variance, not as a single determinism demonstration.

Two conditions bound this extension. The protocol requires active injection
into both sessions: observing natural traffic cannot guarantee that either
party utters the probe set, so the equality verdict is available only where the
detector can issue its own queries to both endpoints. And as with the whole
method, the readout needs top-$k$ log-probabilities; a service that returns only
sampled completions falls outside the framework (limitation~(i)). A quantized
replica served to one user can also register a nonzero divergence against an
unquantized replica ($0.82$ at 4-bit, Table~\ref{tab:quant}), so ``same model''
must be read as ``same served weights,'' not ``same checkpoint after
service-side compression.''

\paragraph{Limitations.}
(i) The method is fundamentally dependent on the service provider exposing
token-level log-probabilities: if an API only returns a final answer, or
quantizes or withholds its top-$k$ log-probabilities, the fingerprint cannot
be extracted. (ii) Latent alignment is necessary but not
sufficient for representation alignment, so probe quality depends on the
candidate-restriction heuristic. (iii) The fingerprint
is tokenizer-relative, so cross-family comparison detects a change of model
family decisively. Tokenizer mismatch drives the
divergence onto its saturated ceiling, bounding fine-grained quantitative
comparison. (iv) The
top-$k$ truncation of the API injects a log-probability floor ($-30$ locally,
$-15$ for the API) below which the next-token distribution is only bounded,
not measured; this
floors the estimated $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ and should be accounted for when setting
detection thresholds.
(v) Our calibration is demonstrated at a single model scale ($0.8$B); whether
the quadratic (Fisher-information) regime holds at much larger scales is
unverified. (vi) An adversary who
controls the served model and knows the method is constrained but not
precluded: second-order stealth is exactly null-space membership
(Prop.~\ref{prop:stealth}), so any distribution-level detector inherits the
output-equivalence ambiguity of \S\ref{sec:intro}. As Remark~\ref{rem:adv}
argues, however, exploiting it requires engineering an edit into a
Jacobian-defined, measure-zero subspace with an exact global compensation
whose norm is bounded below by Eq.~\eqref{eq:compcost}, a computationally
prohibitive task. Randomized or secret probe sets and robust-statistic
variants remain prudent hardening. (vii) The monotone quadratic (Fisher-information) regime of
\S\ref{sec:exp:calib}, which is invertible in the mean, has a finite radius:
beyond the small-perturbation scale the fingerprint saturates toward the entropy
ceiling (\S\ref{sec:theory}, Prop.~\ref{prop:saturation}), and a sufficiently
large perturbation is indistinguishable from a different model, a blind spot
that concerns \emph{attribution} and leaves \emph{detection} intact, just as in the cross-family setting
of \S\ref{sec:exp:cross}. Per-direction
inversion is additionally unreliable where the directional variance is large
(limitation~(ix)).
(viii) The positive case is demonstrated directly: in a locally controlled
environment that simulates the black-box readout, the top-20 truncated
statistic of \S\ref{sec:cov} applied to a served model we control, the detector
successfully identifies both fine-tuning and wholesale model swapping by a wide
margin (\S\ref{sec:mods}): a LoRA fine-tune registers $D_{\mathrm{KL}}=0.15$ and
a Qwen$\to$Llama swap $D_{\mathrm{KL}}=4.11$ (top-$20$), against a $r{=}0.02$
detection floor of $0.016$. The DeepSeek experiment (\S\ref{sec:exp:api})
supplies the zero-noise determinism check over a real API; because a third-party
API exposes no weights to perturb, the same positive control can only be staged
on a self-hosted endpoint, a practical deployment step and a natural next
extension. (ix) The calibration curves of \S\ref{sec:exp:calib} are reported along one
fixed Gaussian direction (seed $0$). Over five directions the relative standard
deviation of $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ is $15$--$40\%$ at small-to-moderate $r$ and
$4$--$10\%$ at saturation (Gemma up to $\approx$ the mean in its transition
regime), so the qualitative conclusions are robust, though the
single-realization values should not be read as precise expectations.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:concl}

We have shown that the per-probe distributional divergence (KL) of next-token
distributions, induced by actively synthesized representation-aligned
probes, is a practical and sensitive fingerprint of a language model's weights.
The approach is black-box-compatible at detection time (requiring only the
top-$k$ log-probabilities of the served model, with probe synthesis performed
once on a local open-weight model), yields a monotonic calibration curve under
injected Gaussian weight noise, and is consistent with a zero-noise determinism
check over a production API. The central finding is conceptual: to detect weight equivalence, one
should compare the \emph{second-order} response of the observable next-token
distribution instead of its expectation. That response is governed by the Fisher
information, and representation-aligned probes, sentences the model's own
representation places together, provide the controlled setting in which that
response is identifiable.

\paragraph{Acknowledgments.} We thank the anonymous reviewers for their
feedback.



\appendix
\section{Theoretical Analysis Details}
\label{sec:theory-app}

We collect here the derivations supporting \S\ref{sec:theory}. For a fixed
probe $\bm{x}$, write $\bm{\phi}_\theta(\bm{x}) \in \R^{|\Vsh|}$ for the
next-token log-probability vector restricted to the shared vocabulary
(Eq.~\eqref{eq:phi}), and define its Jacobian with respect to the weights,
\begin{equation}
  \bm{J}_\theta(\bm{x}) = \frac{\partial \bm{\phi}_\theta(\bm{x})}{\partial \theta}
    \in \R^{|\Vsh| \times P},
  \label{eq:jacobian}
\end{equation}
which is well defined because $\bm{\phi}_\theta$ is a smooth function of the
logits and the logits are smooth functions of $\theta$. Write
$\bm{\phi}_i = \bm{\phi}_\theta(\bm{x}^{(i)})$ and
$\bm{J}_i = \bm{J}_\theta(\bm{x}^{(i)})$.

\subsection{The Jacobian is a score function}
\label{sec:app-score}

\begin{proposition}[The Jacobian is a score function]
\label{prop:score}
Let $z_t(\bm{x}) = [\bm{W}\bm{h}^{(L)}(\bm{x})]_t$ be the logit of token $t$.
Then
\begin{equation}
  \big[\bm{J}_\theta(\bm{x})\big]_{t,:} \;=\;
  \nabla_\theta z_t(\bm{x}) - \sum_{t' \in \V} p_\theta(t' \mid \bm{x})\,\nabla_\theta z_{t'}(\bm{x})
  \;=\; \nabla_\theta \log p_\theta(t \mid \bm{x}),
  \label{eq:score}
\end{equation}
where the subtraction sums over the \emph{full} vocabulary $\V$; it is the
softmax normalizer and is deliberately not restricted to the index set $\Vsh$
over which the vector $\bm{\phi}_\theta$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:phi} is recorded; the
resulting score is then read off at the $\Vsh$-indexed coordinates.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Quadratic response and the Fisher information}
\label{sec:app-fisher}

\begin{proposition}[Quadratic response and the Fisher information]
\label{prop:fisher}
For a fixed probe $\bm{x}$, the KL divergence between the next-token
distributions of $\theta$ and $\theta + \delta$ admits the second-order
expansion
\begin{equation}
  \KL\!\big(p_\theta(\cdot \mid \bm{x})\ \|\ p_{\theta+\delta}(\cdot \mid \bm{x})\big)
  \;=\; \tfrac12\, \delta^\top \bm{F}_\theta(\bm{x})\, \delta
      + O(\norm{\delta}_2^3),
  \label{eq:fisherexpand}
\end{equation}
where the first-order term vanishes identically (since
$\E_{t \sim p_\theta(\cdot \mid \bm{x})}[\nabla_\theta \log p_\theta(t \mid \bm{x})] = 0$)
and
\begin{equation}
  \bm{F}_\theta(\bm{x})
  \;=\; \E_{t \sim p_\theta(\cdot \mid \bm{x})}
      \big[\nabla_\theta \log p_\theta(t \mid \bm{x})
          \,\nabla_\theta \log p_\theta(t \mid \bm{x})^\top\big]
  \;\succeq\; 0
  \label{eq:fisher}
\end{equation}
is the per-probe Fisher information. Averaging over the probe set gives the
probe-averaged Fisher information $\bar{\bm{F}}_\theta = \frac1m \sum_{i=1}^m
\bm{F}_\theta(\bm{x}^{(i)}) \succeq 0$, the coefficient of the mean per-probe
expansion of Eq.~\eqref{eq:dklexpand}. Because $\norm{\delta}_2 \propto r$ under
the random-perturbation protocol of \S\ref{sec:exp:calib}, the expected
divergence grows \emph{quadratically} in $r$ for small perturbations and is
monotone along any fixed perturbation direction. By
Proposition~\ref{prop:score}, $\nabla_\theta \log p_\theta(t \mid \bm{x})$ is
the $t$-th row of $\bm{J}_\theta(\bm{x})$, so
$\bm{F}_\theta(\bm{x}) = \bm{J}_\theta(\bm{x})^\top
\operatorname{diag}\!\big(p_\theta(\cdot \mid \bm{x})\big)
\bm{J}_\theta(\bm{x})$: the Fisher information is a probability-weighted squared
Jacobian. Because $\bar{\bm{F}}_\theta \succeq 0$, its kernel coincides with the
set of directions in which \emph{every} observed per-token score has zero
projection, the same null space $\mathcal{K}_\theta$ that drives the
adversarial-invariance analysis below.
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}
\label{rem:linearity}
Quadratic growth in $r$ and monotonicity hold in expectation and in the
small-perturbation regime of Proposition~\ref{prop:fisher}. The monotonicity of
a single realized calibration curve, in which one fixed noise direction is
rescaled by $r$, is an empirical statement, as in \S\ref{sec:exp:calib}.
Because the realized $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ is, to leading order, the positive
quadratic form $\tfrac12\, r^2 \hat\delta^\top \bar{\bm{F}}_\theta \hat\delta$
with $\bar{\bm{F}}_\theta \succeq 0$, it increases with $r$ along any fixed
direction; the mild sub-quadratic growth at larger $r$ (e.g.\ layer $8$ in
Table~\ref{tab:calib}) is the onset of the saturation regime of
Proposition~\ref{prop:saturation}.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Global Lipschitz property and saturation}
\label{sec:app-sat}

\begin{proposition}[Global Lipschitz property and saturation]
\label{prop:saturation}
For \emph{arbitrary} $\delta$ (not necessarily small), the log-probability
vector is a Lipschitz function of the logits: since the log-softmax has
Jacobian $I - \mathbf{1}\,\softmax(\bm{z})^\top$ of spectral norm at most
$\sqrt{|\V|}$ (ranging from $1$ for a uniform distribution to $\sqrt{|\V|}$ for
a peaked one),
\begin{equation}
  \norm{\bm{\phi}_{\theta+\delta}(\bm{x}) - \bm{\phi}_\theta(\bm{x})}_2
  \;\le\; \sqrt{|\V|}\,\norm{\bm{z}_{\theta+\delta}(\bm{x}) - \bm{z}_\theta(\bm{x})}_2
  \;\le\; \sqrt{|\V|}\,L_{\mathrm{net}}\,\norm{\delta}_2,
  \label{eq:globlip}
\end{equation}
where $L_{\mathrm{net}}$ is the Lipschitz constant of the logit map in the
weights. The boundedness of the divergence, however, does \emph{not} follow
from this Lipschitz inequality, which is valid but non-tight and implies at most
linear growth. It follows from the coordinate bound on $\bm{\phi}$ itself: in the
black-box setting each coordinate lies in the interval $[f, 0]$ (a top-$k$
log-probability is $\le 0$; a floored entry equals the floor $f$), so every
log-probability vector, and hence every per-probe KL divergence, is finite and
uniformly bounded in $\theta$. Unlike the small-scale quadratic regime of
Proposition~\ref{prop:fisher}, however, the divergence does not grow without
bound: it rises to a stable detection ceiling, a boundedness the Lipschitz
estimate does not capture at all. For large $\delta$ the perturbed model's top-$k$ support becomes
asymptotically disjoint from the clean model's (the same phenomenon as
\S\ref{sec:exp:cross}), so the clean model's high-probability tokens are
observed only at the floor $f$; the per-probe KL divergence then approaches
\begin{equation}
  \KL\!\big(p_\theta(\cdot \mid \bm{x}^{(i)})\ \|\ p_{\theta+\delta}(\cdot \mid \bm{x}^{(i)})\big)
  \;\xrightarrow{\ \norm{\delta}_2 \to \infty\ }\;
  -H\big(p_\theta(\cdot \mid \bm{x}^{(i)})\big) - \log f,
  \label{eq:saturation}
\end{equation}
where $H$ denotes Shannon entropy and $f$ is the floor probability ($e^{-30}$
in our local calibration). Averaging over probes, $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ tends to
$\frac1m \sum_i \big[-H(p_\theta(\cdot \mid \bm{x}^{(i)})) - \log f\big]$, a
finite ceiling set by the clean model's entropy and the floor, independent of
any further $\delta$. The $-\log f$ term is floor-dominated: any pair of models
whose observed top-$k$ supports are disjoint saturate at the same uniform cap
$-\log f \approx 30$ regardless of the model, so the only model-dependent part
of the ceiling is the entropy term $-H(p_\theta)$. This is the black-box
counterpart of the fully observable ceiling $\log|\Vsh| - H$ of the white-box
analysis: in the absence of a floor the large-$\delta$ limit is a collapse
toward the uniform distribution over $\Vsh$, whereas the floor replaces that
limit with the disjoint-support one: the perturbed model's top-$k$ support
leaves the clean model's high-probability tokens behind, and they are read at
the floor $f$. A sufficiently large perturbation therefore saturates at the
same maximal divergence as a change of model family. The detection verdict is
decisive: whether the served model is a heavily perturbed relative or a
completely new model, the fingerprint reports the same unambiguous maximal
signal that it is not the reference. Only attribution, the identity of the
distinct model, is left open.
\end{proposition}

Combining Propositions~\ref{prop:fisher} and \ref{prop:saturation}, the
perturbation response passes through three regimes
(Table~\ref{tab:regimes}). The quadratic regime is valid for sufficiently
small $\norm{\delta}_2$; here
$D_{\mathrm{KL}} \approx \tfrac12\, r^2 \hat\delta^\top \bar{\bm{F}}_\theta
\hat\delta$, so the divergence scales as $r^2$ with a coefficient set by the
Fisher information. The intermediate regime is polynomial, with higher-order
terms becoming significant; the saturation regime carries the divergence to a
maximal ceiling set by entropy and the floor. Inverting the monotone
calibration curve of \S\ref{sec:exp:calib}, mapping an observed divergence back
to a perturbation scale, is reliable within the first two regimes, where the
response is strictly increasing in $r$; at saturation the fingerprint has
already registered its strongest signal, the basis of decisive change and
cross-family detection.

\subsection{Adversarial invariance}
\label{sec:app-adv}

A skeptical reader may ask whether an adversary who knows the entire method,
including probe construction and the divergence statistic, can engineer a
targeted weight edit $\delta \in \R^P$ (for instance, injecting a backdoor into
a served model) that evades detection. Formally, let
$\bm{F} : \theta \mapsto \big(p_\theta(\cdot \mid \bm{x}^{(1)}), \dots,
p_\theta(\cdot \mid \bm{x}^{(m)})\big)$ denote the fingerprint map, and suppose
the adversary can reconstruct a probe set close to $\mathcal{P}$, has query
access to top-$k$ log-probabilities, and seeks a perturbation $\delta$ that (i)
implements a target edit supported on a ``backdoor block'' $S \subseteq \{1,
\dots, P\}$, with $\norm{\delta_S}_2 \ge b > 0$ for some minimal strength $b$,
and (ii) leaves the fingerprint invariant, $D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\theta,
\theta+\delta) \approx 0$. Stack the probe Jacobians into
\begin{equation}
  \bm{G}_\theta =
  \begin{bmatrix} \bm{J}_1 \\ \vdots \\ \bm{J}_m \end{bmatrix}
  \in \R^{m|\Vsh| \times P},
  \label{eq:gstack}
\end{equation}
so that the $m$ probe distributions, each a vector in $\R^{|\Vsh|}$, are
observed only through the linear map $\delta \mapsto \bm{G}_\theta\delta$.
Directions in $\mathcal{K}_\theta := \ker \bm{G}_\theta$ are exactly the
directions of vanishing quadratic response of the fingerprint-mismatch
objective $\delta \mapsto D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\theta, \theta+\delta)$: by
Proposition~\ref{prop:fisher}, $\ker \bar{\bm{F}}_\theta = \ker \bm{G}_\theta$
when $\bar{\bm{F}}_\theta$ is the \emph{observed-subset} Fisher information (the
top-$k$ rows of $\bm{G}_\theta$); in the black-box setting the unobserved tail
contributes to the true full-vocabulary Fisher but is dropped from
$\bm{G}_\theta$, so the equality is exact only in white-box and approximate
otherwise. Edits in $\mathcal{K}_\theta$ are invisible to second order by
construction.

\begin{proposition}[Stealth is null-space membership]
\label{prop:stealth}
The KL divergence responds to an edit $\delta$ as
$D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\theta, \theta+\delta) = \tfrac12\, \delta^\top
\bar{\bm{F}}_\theta\, \delta + O(\norm{\delta}_2^3)$
(Proposition~\ref{prop:fisher}). A perturbation is invisible to second
order, meaning the quadratic term vanishes, $\delta^\top \bar{\bm{F}}_\theta\, \delta
= 0$, exactly when $\delta \in \mathcal{K}_\theta = \ker \bm{G}_\theta$, since
$\ker \bar{\bm{F}}_\theta = \ker \bm{G}_\theta$ for the observed-subset Fisher
scoped above (exact in white-box). Moreover, any $\delta \notin
\mathcal{K}_\theta$ carries a \emph{detection floor} that survives the best
possible first-order cancellation: because each \emph{observed} (top-$k$)
next-token probability of the clean model is bounded below by
$\gamma = \min_{i,\, t \in \mathrm{obs}} p_\theta(t \mid \bm{x}^{(i)}) > 0$, the
minimum observed top-$k$ probability, distinct from the log-probability floor $f$
of the truncated tail, the quadratic term obeys
\begin{equation}
  \delta^\top \bar{\bm{F}}_\theta\, \delta
  \;=\; \frac1m \sum_{i=1}^m \sum_{t \in \Vsh}
      p_\theta(t \mid \bm{x}^{(i)})\, \big(\bm{J}_{i,t}\,\delta\big)^2
  \;\ge\; \frac{\gamma}{m}\, \norm{\bm{G}^{\mathrm{obs}}_\theta\,\delta}_2^2,
  \label{eq:floor}
\end{equation}
where $\bm{J}_{i,t}$ is the $t$-th row of $\bm{J}_i$ and
$\bm{G}^{\mathrm{obs}}_\theta$ denotes $\bm{G}_\theta$ restricted to the rows
of the observed (top-$k$) tokens. Because $\gamma$ is the minimum
\emph{observed} probability, this is a conservative lower bound over the
observed, non-floored entries only; the floored tokens contribute nonnegatively
and are omitted. Hence
$D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\theta, \theta+\delta) \ge \frac{\gamma}{2m}\,
\norm{\bm{G}^{\mathrm{obs}}_\theta\,\delta}_2^2 - O(\norm{\delta}_2^3)$. The irreducible term
is quadratic in the \emph{visible} part of $\delta$ and invariant under
$\delta \mapsto -\delta$, so no choice of the first-order response removes it.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[The cost of hiding a visible edit]
\label{prop:comp}
Fix a target edit $\bm{v}_B$ supported on $S$. If $\bm{v}_B \notin
\mathcal{K}_\theta$, any first-order-stealthy perturbation of the form
$\delta = \bm{v}_B + \bm{c}$ must satisfy $\bm{G}_\theta(\bm{v}_B + \bm{c}) =
0$; the minimal-norm compensation is $\bm{c}^\star = -\bm{G}_\theta^{+}
\bm{G}_\theta\,\bm{v}_B = -\Pi_{\mathrm{row}}\,\bm{v}_B$, where
$\Pi_{\mathrm{row}}$ is the orthogonal projection onto the row space of
$\bm{G}_\theta$. Since $\bm{G}_\theta$ annihilates the kernel component of
$\bm{v}_B$, we have $\bm{G}_\theta\bm{v}_B =
\bm{G}_\theta\Pi_{\mathrm{row}}\bm{v}_B$ and hence
\begin{equation}
  \norm{\bm{c}^\star}_2
  \;=\; \norm{\Pi_{\mathrm{row}}\bm{v}_B}_2
  \;\ge\; \frac{\norm{\bm{G}_{\theta,S}\,\bm{v}_B}_2}
                 {\sigma_{\max}\!\big(\bm{G}_\theta\big)},
  \label{eq:compcost}
\end{equation}
where $\bm{G}_{\theta,S}$ restricts $\bm{G}_\theta$ to the columns in $S$ (so
$\bm{G}_\theta\bm{v}_B = \bm{G}_{\theta,S}\bm{v}_B$, since $\bm{v}_B$ is
supported on $S$). This bound makes \emph{no rank assumption}: it is
strictly positive exactly when $\bm{G}_{\theta,S}\bm{v}_B \ne 0$, that is,
whenever the target edit has a nonzero \emph{visible} component within its block
($\bm{v}_B \notin \ker \bm{G}_{\theta,S}$), a condition that holds for
Lebesgue-almost-every $\bm{v}_B$ as soon as
$\operatorname{rank}(\bm{G}_{\theta,S}) \ge 1$. In the fully observable case
$\operatorname{rank}(\bm{G}_{\theta,S}) = |S|$ and
$\sigma_{\min}(\bm{G}_{\theta,S}) > 0$, so
$\norm{\bm{G}_{\theta,S}\bm{v}_B}_2 \ge
\sigma_{\min}(\bm{G}_{\theta,S})\,\norm{\bm{v}_B}_2$ and
Eq.~\eqref{eq:compcost} strengthens to the uniform positive fraction of
$\norm{\bm{v}_B}_2$; the rank-free bound is thus a strict generalization of the
rank-based one. In particular, a \emph{support-confined} edit
($\delta = \delta_S$) is stealthy only if $\delta_S \in \ker
\bm{G}_{\theta,S}$, a subspace of dimension $|S| -
\operatorname{rank}(\bm{G}_{\theta,S})$: full rank collapses it to $\{0\}$ (no
nonzero support-confined stealthy edit), while in general it remains a proper,
generically thin subspace, and any edit outside it forces the adversary to
perturb weights outside $S$ with a compensation norm bounded below by its
visible footprint scaled by $1/\sigma_{\max}(\bm{G}_\theta)$, thereby inflating
both the total edit norm and, through Eq.~\eqref{eq:floor}, its second-order
footprint.
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}[Why evasion is computationally prohibitive]
\label{rem:adv}
Propositions~\ref{prop:stealth} and \ref{prop:comp} make precise the sense in
which ``keeping the distributional-divergence fingerprint invariant across
multiple probes'' is possible in principle but computationally prohibitive in
practice, consistent with Limitation~(vi) of \S\ref{sec:disc}, which concedes
that evasion is not precluded. The kernel $\mathcal{K}_\theta$ has codimension
$\operatorname{rank}\bm{G}_\theta \le m|\Vsh|$, and since $P$ exceeds
$m|\Vsh|$ by orders of magnitude for the models considered, it is nonempty: the
obstacle is the thinness of the invariant directions, the
ill-conditioned nonlinear system one must solve to exploit them, and the exact
global compensation their use requires. (i) \emph{Thinness.} The set of
target edits satisfying $\bm{v}_B \in \mathcal{K}_\theta$ is a proper linear
subspace, hence of measure zero in $\R^P$; a backdoor not engineered
specifically for the kernel is visible with certainty. (ii)
\emph{Discoverability.} Engineering an invariant edit requires the Jacobian,
and, because the invariance condition $D_{\mathrm{KL}} \approx 0$ is a
\emph{nonlinear} constraint on $\theta$, of which the quadratic form of
Proposition~\ref{prop:fisher} is only the small-$\delta$ approximation, one
must solve an ill-conditioned nonlinear system. White-box, one must solve such
a system of $m|\Vsh|$ equations in $P \sim 10^9$ variables. The
loss landscape of an LLM is pathologically ill-conditioned. At the level
of the observable, the singular values of $\bm{G}_\theta$ span many decades; by
Proposition~\ref{prop:score} the Fisher information is $\sum_i \bm{J}_i^\top
\operatorname{diag}\!\big(p_\theta(\cdot\mid \bm{x}^{(i)})\big)\bm{J}_i$, so
the curvature along a weight direction is a probability-weighted squared
observable footprint. Any finite-precision solve leaves a residue
$\bm{G}_\theta\hat\delta \ne 0$ that Eq.~\eqref{eq:floor} amplifies into an
irreducible quadratic shift. Black-box, which is the setting this paper
targets, $\bm{G}_\theta$ is not even available: estimating it by forward
differences costs $\Omega(P)$ noisy oracle queries, and any ``null'' direction
$\hat\delta$ of the estimated Jacobian $\hat{\bm{G}}$ satisfies
$\norm{\bm{G}_\theta\hat\delta}_2 \le \norm{\hat{\bm{G}} - \bm{G}_\theta}
\,\norm{\hat\delta}_2$, again leaving a floor for the detector. (iii)
\emph{Cost of use.} Even when an invariant direction exists,
Eq.~\eqref{eq:compcost} shows that hiding a behavior-relevant edit requires a
\emph{global} compensation whose norm is at least the edit's visible footprint
scaled by $1/\sigma_{\max}(\bm{G}_\theta)$, positive whenever the block is not
entirely invisible to the fingerprint (that is, whenever
$\operatorname{rank}(\bm{G}_{\theta,S}) \ge 1$), and the compensation must be
\emph{exact}: any shortfall is read by the second-order floor. Taken together,
these three costs, a measure-zero target subspace, an ill-conditioned
nonlinear solve, and an exact global compensation, make successful evasion
computationally prohibitive for the models considered, even though it is not
ruled out in principle.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Quantitative size of the stealth region]
\label{rem:advscale}
The floor of Eq.~\eqref{eq:floor} is quadratic in the visible residue, so the
effective radius of the stealth region shrinks as the fingerprint sensitivity
grows. Under the random-perturbation protocol of \S\ref{sec:exp:calib}
($\delta \sim \mathcal{N}(0, (r\sigma)^2 I)$),
Proposition~\ref{prop:fisher} gives the expected KL shift as
$\tfrac12 (r\sigma)^2 \operatorname{tr}\bar{\bm{F}}_\theta$, and requiring
$D_{\mathrm{KL}} \le \tau$ pins the perturbation scale to
$r\sigma \lesssim \sqrt{2\tau \big/ \operatorname{tr}\bar{\bm{F}}_\theta}$.
The measured sensitivities are large. $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ reaches
$0.010$--$0.028$ already at $r = 0.02$ and grows as $r^2$
(Table~\ref{tab:calib}), so this radius is small compared with any edit that
materially changes model behavior. The second-order incompressibility of
Proposition~\ref{prop:stealth} is what converts a fingerprint that is merely
\emph{sensitive} into one that is \emph{costly to evade}.
\end{remark}
